	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Nitrogen (N) is the most abundant constituent of the atmosphere (78.09%).
Nitrogen enters the atmosphere from volcanoes, and from the decay of organic
matter. It is removed from the atmosphere by nitrogen-fixing bacteria. Nitrogen
compounds are very reactive and play integral roles in the production and
destruction of ozone in the atmosphere.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
