</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/18d9ddbb-66cc-4b92-aa8d-2395ab3a17ce">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/b9c56939-c624-467d-b196-e56a5b660334"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2021-03-04 08:25:32.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Noble gases makes up a class of chemical elements with similar properties; under standard conditions, they are all odorless, colorless, monatomic gases with very low chemical reactivity. The six naturally occurring noble gases are helium (He), neon (Ne), argon (Ar), krypton (Kr), xenon (Xe), and the radioactive radon (Rn). Oganesson (Og) is variously predicted to be a noble gas as well or to break the trend due to relativistic effects; its chemistry has not yet been investigated.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2021-02-26 09:47:26.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (NOBLE GAS [18d9ddbb-66cc-4b92-aa8d-2395ab3a17ce,560876] - ATMOSPHERIC CHEMISTRY [b9c56939-c624-467d-b196-e56a5b660334,83481]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2021-02-26 12:41:17.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (NOBLE GAS [18d9ddbb-66cc-4b92-aa8d-2395ab3a17ce,560876] - ATMOSPHERIC RADON [a81f6b17-2a16-4600-84b6-bcbcc5c29d2f,560877]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Noble gases makes up a class of chemical elements with similar properties; under standard conditions, they are all odorless, colorless, monatomic gases with very low chemical reactivity. The six naturally occurring noble gases are helium (He), neon (Ne), argon (Ar), krypton (Kr), xenon (Xe), and the radioactive radon (Rn). Oganesson (Og) is variously predicted to be a noble gas as well or to break the trend due to relativistic effects; its chemistry has not yet been investigated.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">NOBLE GAS</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1152"/>
	<narrower xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a81f6b17-2a16-4600-84b6-bcbcc5c29d2f"/>
</rdf:Description>

