	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Ground that freezes and thaws annually.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">SEASONALLY FROZEN GROUND</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx171"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/8073b62d-a2f3-4ad9-b619-de26f28877a7">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/6a426480-c58f-4b6b-8e35-0975b7f6edb5"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2017-03-10 08:54:56.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (Soil within which the moisture has predominantly changed to ice, the unfrozen portion being in vapor phase. 
Ice within the soil bonds adjacent soil particles and renders frozen ground very hard. Permanently frozen ground is called permafrost. Dry frozen ground is relatively loose and crumbly because of the lack of bonding
ice. Frozen ground is sometimes in-advisedly called frost or ground frost.); 
update Definition (American Meteorological Society Glossary of Meteorology Online "http://amsglossary.allenpress.com/glossary" ); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Soil within which the moisture has predominantly changed to ice, the unfrozen portion being in vapor phase. 
Ice within the soil bonds adjacent soil particles and renders frozen ground very hard. Permanently frozen ground is called permafrost. Dry frozen ground is relatively loose and crumbly because of the lack of bonding
ice. Frozen ground is sometimes in-advisedly called frost or ground frost.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">FROZEN GROUND</prefLabel>
