	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Maps, either static or dynamic, developed to illuminate geographic areas that are affected by or vulnerable to a particular hazard. When properly utilized by decision makers, hazards mapping can be used to save lives and reduce economic losses by limiting or avoiding exposure to a hazard. Hazards maps can be made for natural hazards, such as earthquakes and floods, as well as technological hazards like groundwater contamination and gas leaks.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">HAZARD MAPPING</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2768"/>
