	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 11:07:11.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (The degree to which an electrical current can pass through water due to ions dissolved in the water. Salinity is often calculated from conductivity and temperature.); 
update Definition (Adapted from: Dictionary of Earth Science, 5th ed.. 1997.  Sybil Parker, ed. in chief. McGraw-Hill.  ); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">The degree to which an electrical current can pass through water due to ions dissolved in the water. Salinity is often calculated from conductivity and temperature.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
