<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/0b5e5a9b-5552-4e41-b1a1-9c01c52dff4b">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/3ab3aa92-9cca-4660-a0ed-281fff07eede"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-08-20 10:14:16.0 [tbs1979]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: The movement of solid particles (sediment), typically due to a combination of the force of gravity acting on the sediment, and/or the movement of the fluid in which the sediment is entrained. An understanding of sediment transport is typically used in natural systems, where the particles are clastic rocks (sand, gravel, boulders, etc.), mud, or clay; the fluid is air, water, or ice; and the force of gravity acts to move the particles due to the sloping surface on which they are resting.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-08-05 14:13:53.0 [tbs1979] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (SEDIMENT TRANSPORT [0b5e5a9b-5552-4e41-b1a1-9c01c52dff4b,105445] - LOESS [5ce16b97-c91c-420c-9701-33d19d50b286,105449]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-08-05 14:14:17.0 [tbs1979] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (SEDIMENT TRANSPORT [0b5e5a9b-5552-4e41-b1a1-9c01c52dff4b,105445] - MONADNOCK [abe3a81a-3bac-450b-8006-304bee055289,105451]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-08-05 14:13:09.0 [tbs1979] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (SEDIMENT TRANSPORT [0b5e5a9b-5552-4e41-b1a1-9c01c52dff4b,105445] - AEOLIAN PROCESSES [3ab3aa92-9cca-4660-a0ed-281fff07eede,84207]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">The movement of solid particles (sediment), typically due to a combination of the force of gravity acting on the sediment, and/or the movement of the fluid in which the sediment is entrained. An understanding of sediment transport is typically used in natural systems, where the particles are clastic rocks (sand, gravel, boulders, etc.), mud, or clay; the fluid is air, water, or ice; and the force of gravity acts to move the particles due to the sloping surface on which they are resting.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">SEDIMENT TRANSPORT</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2873"/>
