<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a85b812e-e4d2-4dce-bf67-d89a3e1a9122">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/b29b46ad-f05f-4144-b965-5f606ce96963"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2012-06-25 09:43:52.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (TROPICAL OR EXTRATROPICAL CYCLONE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY [a85b812e-e4d2-4dce-bf67-d89a3e1a9122,39661] - EXTREME WEATHER [b29b46ad-f05f-4144-b965-5f606ce96963,39651]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2024-09-23 10:42:55.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Refers to the frequency and intensity of tropical or extratropical cyclones. The term tropical cyclone (TC) encompasses hurricanes of the Western Hemisphere and their typhoon and cyclone equivalents elsewhere. At maturity, the TC is one of the most intense and feared storms in the world; winds exceeding 90 m s−1 (175 knots; kt) have been measured, and both its rains and storm surge can cause great loss of life and damage. The term extratropical cyclone refers to any cyclonic-scale storm that is not a tropical cyclone, usually referring only to the migratory frontal cyclones of middle and high latitudes.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Refers to the frequency and intensity of tropical or extratropical cyclones. The term tropical cyclone (TC) encompasses hurricanes of the Western Hemisphere and their typhoon and cyclone equivalents elsewhere. At maturity, the TC is one of the most intense and feared storms in the world; winds exceeding 90 m s−1 (175 knots; kt) have been measured, and both its rains and storm surge can cause great loss of life and damage. The term extratropical cyclone refers to any cyclonic-scale storm that is not a tropical cyclone, usually referring only to the migratory frontal cyclones of middle and high latitudes.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">TROPICAL OR EXTRATROPICAL CYCLONE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1401"/>
</rdf:Description>

