<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/9ab53717-17f1-4259-b425-0eed19c31884">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:52:50.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (CHROMIUM [9ab53717-17f1-4259-b425-0eed19c31884,310655] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:20:41.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A lustrous, brittle, hard metal. Its color is silver-gray and it can be highly polished. It does not tarnish in air. When heated it borns and forms the green chromic oxide. Chromium is unstable in oxygen. It immediately produces a thin oxide layer that is impermeable to oxygen and protects the metal below.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A lustrous, brittle, hard metal. Its color is silver-gray and it can be highly polished. It does not tarnish in air. When heated it borns and forms the green chromic oxide. Chromium is unstable in oxygen. It immediately produces a thin oxide layer that is impermeable to oxygen and protects the metal below.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">CHROMIUM</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx3167"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/160cce6b-c9f5-45bf-9a51-ee477f446cce">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:38:16.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (A chemical element and a lustrous transition metal with a silver color, low density, and high strength. Titanium is resistant to corrosion in sea water, aqua regia, and chlorine.); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:54:33.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (TITANIUM [160cce6b-c9f5-45bf-9a51-ee477f446cce,310667] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:37:51.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A chemical element and a lustrous transition metal with a silver color, low density, and high strength. Titanium is resistant to corrosion in sea water, aqua regia, and chlorine. [
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A chemical element and a lustrous transition metal with a silver color, low density, and high strength. Titanium is resistant to corrosion in sea water, aqua regia, and chlorine.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">TITANIUM</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1609"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/0965eb7b-6bd3-48a3-aa2a-52d7e2dda8ad">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:53:08.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (IRON [0965eb7b-6bd3-48a3-aa2a-52d7e2dda8ad,310659] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:26:01.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A metal in the first transition series. It is by mass the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most common element in the Earth's crust. Its abundance in rocky planets like Earth is due to its abundant production by fusion in high-mass stars, where it is the last element to be produced with release of energy before the violent collapse of a supernova, which scatters the iron into space.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A metal in the first transition series. It is by mass the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most common element in the Earth's crust. Its abundance in rocky planets like Earth is due to its abundant production by fusion in high-mass stars, where it is the last element to be produced with release of energy before the violent collapse of a supernova, which scatters the iron into space.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">IRON</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1625"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ab12b3d6-2cbf-4a5a-a410-1d23afe906d8">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:54:54.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (ZINC [ab12b3d6-2cbf-4a5a-a410-1d23afe906d8,310671] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:40:32.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A chemical element and the 24th most abundant element in Earth's crust and has five stable isotopes. The most common zinc ore is sphalerite (zinc blende), a zinc sulfide mineral. The largest workable lodes are in Australia, Asia, and the United States. Zinc is refined by froth flotation of the ore, roasting, and final extraction using electricity (electrowinning).
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A chemical element and the 24th most abundant element in Earth's crust and has five stable isotopes. The most common zinc ore is sphalerite (zinc blende), a zinc sulfide mineral. The largest workable lodes are in Australia, Asia, and the United States. Zinc is refined by froth flotation of the ore, roasting, and final extraction using electricity (electrowinning).</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">ZINC</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2346"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/6fff6994-a0d8-4f19-8d36-c9f354b08b19">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:55:45.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Small unicellular organisms lacking a nucleus and some other eukaryotic organelles, they may have photosynthetic pigments but lack chloroplasts, the specialized photosynthetic organelles in higher plants, and mitochondria.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-06 13:08:22.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (PATHOGEN [6fff6994-a0d8-4f19-8d36-c9f354b08b19,310637] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Small unicellular organisms lacking a nucleus and some other eukaryotic organelles, they may have photosynthetic pigments but lack chloroplasts, the specialized photosynthetic organelles in higher plants, and mitochondria.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">PATHOGEN</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1850"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/3e666b9f-6cf5-4454-9a65-987e981cd80e">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:52:12.0 [tstevens] Move Concepts 
delete broader relation (null); 
add broader relation (BARIUM [3e666b9f-6cf5-4454-9a65-987e981cd80e,310651] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:17:03.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A silvery-white metal that can be found in the environment, where it exists naturally. It occurs combined with other chemicals, such as sulfur, carbon or oxygen. It is very light and its density is half that of iron. Barium oxidizes in air, reacts vigorously with water to form the hydroxide, liberating hydrogen. Barium reacts with almost all the non-metals, forming often poisoning compounds.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:49:13.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (BARIUM [3e666b9f-6cf5-4454-9a65-987e981cd80e,310651] - METALS/MINERALS [961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12,310629]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A silvery-white metal that can be found in the environment, where it exists naturally. It occurs combined with other chemicals, such as sulfur, carbon or oxygen. It is very light and its density is half that of iron. Barium oxidizes in air, reacts vigorously with water to form the hydroxide, liberating hydrogen. Barium reacts with almost all the non-metals, forming often poisoning compounds.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">BARIUM</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2170"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/e848dbd1-b70a-4820-a630-98bd642ae357">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-06 13:43:15.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (Dissolved or particulate substances that are not hydrocarbons or hydrocarbon derivatives. Variables such as dissolved inorganic matter (DIM) or particulate inorganic matter (PIM) are included as detailed variables under inorganic matter.); 
update Definition (Webster's College Dictionary. 1991. Random House.); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-06 13:05:22.0 [tstevens] Move Concepts 
delete broader relation (null); 
add broader relation (INORGANIC MATTER [e848dbd1-b70a-4820-a630-98bd642ae357,86787] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Dissolved or particulate substances that are not hydrocarbons or hydrocarbon derivatives. Variables such as dissolved inorganic matter (DIM) or particulate inorganic matter (PIM) are included as detailed variables under inorganic matter.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">INORGANIC MATTER</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2617"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:54:40.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (Minerals are solid, naturally occurring inorganic substances that can be found in the earth’s crust. They are formed without the intervention of humans and have a definite chemical composition and crystal structure.

Metals are elementary substances, such as gold, silver and copper that are crystalline when solid and naturally occurring in minerals. They often have the characteristics of being good conductors of electricity and heat, of being shiny in appearance and of being malleable.); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:54:08.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Minerals are solid, naturally occurring inorganic substances that can be found in the earth’s crust. They are formed without the intervention of humans and have a definite chemical composition and crystal structure.

Metals are elementary substances, such as gold, silver and copper that are crystalline when solid and naturally occurring in minerals. They often have the characteristics of being good conductors of electricity and heat, of being shiny in appearance and of being malleable
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:49:13.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (METALS/MINERALS [961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12,310629] - BARIUM [3e666b9f-6cf5-4454-9a65-987e981cd80e,310651]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:48:52.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (METALS/MINERALS [961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12,310629] - ARSENIC [fa892a9e-523b-424e-bf02-1a8d1e618985,310649]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:51:59.0 [tstevens] Move Concepts 
delete narrower relation (null); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:52:30.0 [tstevens] Move Concepts 
delete broader relation (null); 
add broader relation (METALS/MINERALS [961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12,310629] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-06 13:01:45.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (METALS/MINERALS [961591ce-9207-47db-9aeb-11586371fa12,310629] - WATER QUALITY/WATER CHEMISTRY [8c02f5d1-ce86-4bf5-84d5-b3496cdba6ad,83565]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:52:12.0 [tstevens] Move Concepts 
delete narrower relation (null); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Minerals are solid, naturally occurring inorganic substances that can be found in the earth’s crust. They are formed without the intervention of humans and have a definite chemical composition and crystal structure.

Metals are elementary substances, such as gold, silver and copper that are crystalline when solid and naturally occurring in minerals. They often have the characteristics of being good conductors of electricity and heat, of being shiny in appearance and of being malleable.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">METALS/MINERALS</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2363"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/072721be-eb8b-4ac4-9354-251dbf74ade0">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:34:46.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A chemical element which, in nature,  occurs only in ionic salts. Elemental potassium is a soft silvery-white alkali metal that oxidizes rapidly in air and reacts vigorously with water, generating sufficient heat to ignite hydrogen emitted in the reaction and burning with a lilac-colored flame. It is found dissolved in sea water and is part of many minerals.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:53:40.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (POTASSIUM [072721be-eb8b-4ac4-9354-251dbf74ade0,310663] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A chemical element which, in nature,  occurs only in ionic salts. Elemental potassium is a soft silvery-white alkali metal that oxidizes rapidly in air and reacts vigorously with water, generating sufficient heat to ignite hydrogen emitted in the reaction and burning with a lilac-colored flame. It is found dissolved in sea water and is part of many minerals.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">POTASSIUM</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1819"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/b2318fb3-788c-4f36-a1d1-36670d2da747">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 07:53:59.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (SELENIUM [b2318fb3-788c-4f36-a1d1-36670d2da747,310665] - CONTAMINANTS [42c6d91b-afef-4638-95c5-0d130828b2e7,84623]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2018-03-07 09:35:46.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A chemical element, a nonmetal, that rarely occurs in its elemental state or as pure ore compounds in the Earth's crust. Selenium is found in metal sulfide ores, where it partially replaces the sulfur.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A chemical element, a nonmetal, that rarely occurs in its elemental state or as pure ore compounds in the Earth's crust. Selenium is found in metal sulfide ores, where it partially replaces the sulfur.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">SELENIUM</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2573"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1431">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<text xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" xml:lang="en">Rand, Gary M. 1995. Fundamentals of Aquatic Toxicology: Effects, Environmental Fate, and Risk Assessment.  Taylor and Francis. Washington, D.C.</text>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/2111d240-315c-411b-8114-7ef9e89317e5">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/8c4e2397-aed6-4ce4-9ead-08323e2f90ae"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2024-09-23 10:27:59.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Refers to amount (increased or decreased) of cloud fraction, which is the measurement used to determine how much of the Earth is covered by clouds. The measurement is important because clouds play a large role in regulating the amount of energy that reaches the Earth from the sun as well as the amount of energy that the Earth reflects and emits back into space.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2012-06-25 09:28:59.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (INCREASED/DECREASED CLOUD FRACTION [2111d240-315c-411b-8114-7ef9e89317e5,39649] - CLOUD INDICATORS [8c4e2397-aed6-4ce4-9ead-08323e2f90ae,39647]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Refers to amount (increased or decreased) of cloud fraction, which is the measurement used to determine how much of the Earth is covered by clouds. The measurement is important because clouds play a large role in regulating the amount of energy that reaches the Earth from the sun as well as the amount of energy that the Earth reflects and emits back into space.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">INCREASED/DECREASED CLOUD FRACTION</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2019"/>
</rdf:Description>

