	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2022-03-03 17:49:15.0 [sritz] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (HYDROCHLOROFLUOROCARBONS [f6b97280-74d0-4233-bd17-f9f3d9dd21c2,833689] - HCFC-22 [f2464ebf-811a-43c2-bd50-9e80e03e07b3,836069]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">HCFCs, with one or more hydrogen-carbon bonds  are slated to replace CFCs&#xD;
in most of the current applications. HCFCs are more unstable and subject&#xD;
to hydroxyl radical and  ozone attack early in their gas phase career in&#xD;
the atmosphere. Therefore, their atmospheric lifetime is projected to be&#xD;
shorter  and their chance of reaching the stratosphere&#xD;
decreased. [Spectator; v 272; p 9-11; 1994][New Scientist; v 141; p 6-7;&#xD;
1994.]</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">HYDROCHLOROFLUOROCARBONS</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1519"/>
	<narrower xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/f2464ebf-811a-43c2-bd50-9e80e03e07b3"/>
</rdf:Description>

<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/f2464ebf-811a-43c2-bd50-9e80e03e07b3">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/f6b97280-74d0-4233-bd17-f9f3d9dd21c2"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2022-03-03 17:49:15.0 [sritz] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (HCFC-22 [f2464ebf-811a-43c2-bd50-9e80e03e07b3,836069] - HYDROCHLOROFLUOROCARBONS [f6b97280-74d0-4233-bd17-f9f3d9dd21c2,833689]); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">Chlorodifluoromethane or difluoromonochloromethane is a hydrochlorofluorocarbon (HCFC). This colorless gas is better known as HCFC-22, or R-22, or CHClF It is commonly used as a propellant and refrigerant. These applications are being phased out in developed countries due to the compound's ozone depletion potential (ODP) and high global warming potential (GWP), although global use of R-22 continues to increase because of high demand in developing countries</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">HCFC-22</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx2191"/>
</rdf:Description>

