<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/e1035388-6993-4143-966b-30ced627c2da">
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#Resource"/>
	<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
	<broader xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/7c4d5f8f-4809-4859-b379-3b8c379bc83c"/>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-02-14 09:40:16.0 [mpmorahan] Move Concepts 
add broader relation (CUMULONIMBUS CALVUS [e1035388-6993-4143-966b-30ced627c2da,82211] - CUMULONIMBUS [7c4d5f8f-4809-4859-b379-3b8c379bc83c,82205]); 
delete broader relation (null); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-03-20 18:49:50.0 [mpmorahan]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A species of cumulonimbus cloud evolving from cumulus congestus.

The protuberances of its upper portion have begun to lose their cumuliform outline; they loom and usually flatten, then transform into a whitish mass with more or less diffuse outlines and vertical striations. Cirriform cloud is not present, but the transformation into ice crystals often proceeds with great rapidity. Most often, this cloud is accompanied by showers. By convention, the name cumulonimbus calvus is given to any highly developed cumuliform cloud that produces lightning, thunder, or hail, although the top shows no obvious trace of transformation into ice. The species calvus is unique to the genus cumulonimbus.
language code: en); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-03-20 18:43:45.0 [mpmorahan] Move Concepts 
add narrower relation (CUMULONIMBUS CALVUS [e1035388-6993-4143-966b-30ced627c2da,82211] - CUMULONIMBUS CAPILLATUS [eec3cec2-1649-4507-b91d-3a25ab2200ee,82207]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-02-14 09:40:00.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (CUMULONIMBUS CALVUS [e1035388-6993-4143-966b-30ced627c2da,82211] - CUMULONIMBUS CAPILLATUS [eec3cec2-1649-4507-b91d-3a25ab2200ee,82207]); 
</changeNote>
	<changeNote xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2013-03-20 18:44:03.0 [mpmorahan] Move Concepts 
delete narrower relation (null); 
</changeNote>
	<definition xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">A species of cumulonimbus cloud evolving from cumulus congestus.

The protuberances of its upper portion have begun to lose their cumuliform outline; they loom and usually flatten, then transform into a whitish mass with more or less diffuse outlines and vertical striations. Cirriform cloud is not present, but the transformation into ice crystals often proceeds with great rapidity. Most often, this cloud is accompanied by showers. By convention, the name cumulonimbus calvus is given to any highly developed cumuliform cloud that produces lightning, thunder, or hail, although the top shows no obvious trace of transformation into ice. The species calvus is unique to the genus cumulonimbus.</definition>
	<inScheme xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" rdf:resource="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords"/>
	<prefLabel xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xml:lang="en">CUMULONIMBUS CALVUS</prefLabel>
	<reference xmlns="https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms#" rdf:nodeID="node1igi5qapbx1831"/>
</rdf:Description>

